{
    "background_story": "Jackson was a humble artist who never sought to draw attention to himself. Despite his incredible talent, he consistently undersold his work, preferring to focus on the creative process rather than the financial gain. His paintings were masterpieces, yet he would often sell them for a fraction of their worth, simply because he wanted to share his art with the world. As a result, many of his pieces ended up in the hands of collectors who had no idea of their true value. Jackson's friends and family would often try to convince him to raise his prices, but he would just smile and say that he was happy to see his art bring joy to others. It wasn't until years later, when his work was rediscovered by a prominent art critic, that Jackson's talent was truly recognized, and his paintings were valued for their true worth.",
